1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,750
This is a PowerWash Simulator development demonstration.

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:08,430
Watch the cleaning tool and the playground separately.

3
00:00:08,660 --> 00:00:11,810
As the aim sweeps across the floor, the background moves too.

4
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:13,870
The next shot behaves differently.

5
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:16,735
Posts and floor boundaries stay in roughly the same screen

6
00:00:16,751 --> 00:00:20,570
positions, while the nozzle and aiming point move within the image.

7
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:23,130
The developer calls this Aim Mode.

8
00:00:23,220 --> 00:00:26,247
Separating the viewing direction from tool aiming reduces the

9
00:00:26,263 --> 00:00:29,207
need to turn the whole image while following a small target.

10
00:00:29,223 --> 00:00:32,350
Watch how tool aiming connects to background movement.

11
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:35,990
The same task can involve different amounts of whole-view rotation.

12
00:00:36,340 --> 00:00:38,850
This footage shows a development version from 2022.

13
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:42,490
It is not a comparison of every current setting
or individual motion-sickness responses.

14
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:45,787
We will separate necessary view movement from aiming, and examine

15
00:00:45,803 --> 00:00:48,530
camera design that gives players control over that relationship.

16
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:00,730
The game image can travel and turn while the player remains seated.

17
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:06,070
Conflict between visual and bodily motion signals
is one explanatory theory of motion sickness.

18
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:09,870
People do not all respond in the same way to a moving camera.

19
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:13,970
Someone else being comfortable does not make your discomfort disappear.

20
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:17,770
Our focus is a developer choice, not treatment.

21
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:24,407
Separating movement needed for control from movement added
for presentation helps identify what players could adjust.

22
00:01:24,423 --> 00:01:30,250
Think of the left side as turning toward a target,
and the right side as extra shake layered on top.

23
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:33,840
The first design question is whether these should be controlled separately.

24
00:01:34,567 --> 00:01:37,657
Return to the Aim Mode demonstration.

25
00:01:37,847 --> 00:01:41,334
The nozzle moves toward the left and right of the image,

26
00:01:41,350 --> 00:01:44,257
while the stairs and trees remain in place for a time.

27
00:01:44,427 --> 00:01:47,014
The aiming position on the floor is

28
00:01:47,030 --> 00:01:49,577
separated from the direction of the view.

29
00:01:49,687 --> 00:01:53,497
Moving the tool and rotating the whole image are different events.

30
00:01:53,667 --> 00:01:55,894
At the edge, the view turns and

31
00:01:55,910 --> 00:01:58,084
the roundabout comes into view.

32
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:00,134
Aim Mode does not mean that the camera

33
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:01,917
is completely fixed at all times.

34
00:02:02,107 --> 00:02:06,134
Now watch the roundabout being cleaned. Follow the surface

35
00:02:06,150 --> 00:02:09,794
reached by the spray, rather than the distant trees. The

36
00:02:09,810 --> 00:02:12,677
task continues during stretches with less view rotation.

37
00:02:13,267 --> 00:02:16,597
Separating aim still requires a way to look elsewhere.

38
00:02:16,787 --> 00:02:19,374
Designers need to consider how aiming toward an

39
00:02:19,390 --> 00:02:21,937
edge connects to looking around the environment.

40
00:02:22,147 --> 00:02:25,737
The demonstration shows how a task connects to a viewpoint.

41
00:02:25,867 --> 00:02:28,354
It does not show that the option is

42
00:02:28,370 --> 00:02:30,257
equally comfortable for everyone.

43
00:02:37,133 --> 00:02:40,683
Removing all camera movement would prevent looking elsewhere.

44
00:02:40,813 --> 00:02:47,483
Instead, consider view-turning input, tool
aiming and walking bob as separate layers.

45
00:02:47,753 --> 00:02:51,760
Vertical bob added to suggest walking and shake added to emphasize

46
00:02:51,776 --> 00:02:55,863
impact do not serve the same purpose as facing a target.

47
00:02:55,953 --> 00:02:57,623
Their adjustment controls can be separate too.

48
00:02:57,713 --> 00:03:02,423
Accessibility guidance recommends player
control over these additional movements.

49
00:03:02,633 --> 00:03:07,150
Having an option and achieving comfort for an individual are separate questions.

50
00:03:07,166 --> 00:03:13,240
In this diagram, view rotation remains available
while extra shake is reduced. In implementation,

51
00:03:13,256 --> 00:03:18,943
separate controls by responsibility so one switch
does not unexpectedly change tool aiming or movement.

52
00:03:19,667 --> 00:03:22,697
This is another part of the playground task.

53
00:03:22,827 --> 00:03:25,454
When the player moves sideways between posts, the positions

54
00:03:25,470 --> 00:03:28,417
of nearby posts and distant trees change together.

55
00:03:28,667 --> 00:03:31,717
If the surface is hidden, the viewing position may need to change.

56
00:03:32,047 --> 00:03:34,754
Removing that movement and separating aim

57
00:03:34,770 --> 00:03:37,317
after taking a position are different choices.

58
00:03:37,447 --> 00:03:39,834
In the upper-structure shot, the view tilts

59
00:03:39,850 --> 00:03:42,677
upward and the spray reaches a higher surface.

60
00:03:42,767 --> 00:03:46,637
A target moving from the floor to overhead geometry
can require a different viewing direction.

61
00:03:46,657 --> 00:03:49,314
On another surface, the nozzle follows

62
00:03:49,330 --> 00:03:51,477
posts and the broad surfaces beside them.

63
00:03:51,867 --> 00:03:54,854
Distinguish taking a position from cleaning a

64
00:03:54,870 --> 00:03:57,037
surface while facing roughly the same direction.

65
00:03:57,427 --> 00:03:59,714
Even if reducing screen movement is a goal,

66
00:03:59,730 --> 00:04:01,857
hidden surfaces must remain discoverable.

67
00:04:02,187 --> 00:04:04,894
An aiming option can provide another way to perform

68
00:04:04,910 --> 00:04:07,277
the task rather than remove the task itself.

69
00:04:07,567 --> 00:04:09,817
These are excerpts from different moments.

70
00:04:10,267 --> 00:04:12,894
They are not a controlled repetition of identical inputs, so begin

71
00:04:12,910 --> 00:04:15,357
by describing the visible relationship between movement and aim.

72
00:04:22,117 --> 00:04:29,347
A single menu item called screen movement
makes it hard to know what changing it does.

73
00:04:29,497 --> 00:04:33,607
Explain view sensitivity, tool aiming and extra shake separately.

74
00:04:33,857 --> 00:04:37,587
Sensitivity concerns how much the view turns for an input.

75
00:04:37,697 --> 00:04:41,227
Aim Mode concerns the relationship between aim and view.

76
00:04:41,437 --> 00:04:43,367
Those terms are not interchangeable.

77
00:04:43,597 --> 00:04:46,247
This option list is a design proposal.

78
00:04:46,257 --> 00:04:49,874
It does not mean the development demonstration contains this exact menu.

79
00:04:49,890 --> 00:04:53,347
Distinguish the actual game from our proposed interface.

80
00:04:53,737 --> 00:04:59,507
A short explanation and a reset let players inspect
a difference and return to the original value.

81
00:04:59,837 --> 00:05:04,967
Explaining the choice comes before choosing
one recommended setting for everyone.

82
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,870
This is official gameplay footage of The Talos Principle 2.

83
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:12,207
Watch platform movement, then the view

84
00:05:12,223 --> 00:05:14,470
turning toward overhead geometry.

85
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:17,810
In another shot, walls and lasers tilt.

86
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:20,650
The orientation of the puzzle view changes.

87
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:23,267
Changing space or looking in a new

88
00:05:23,283 --> 00:05:25,970
direction can be connected to game rules.

89
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:29,010
These separate shots are not continuous play.

90
00:05:29,220 --> 00:05:32,290
The important question is whether the new orientation remains understandable.

91
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:34,957
Return to a different surface in the cleaning game.

92
00:05:34,973 --> 00:05:39,027
When looking up beneath the structure and then toward another face,

93
00:05:39,043 --> 00:05:43,230
nearby planks and posts help identify the viewing direction after a turn.

94
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:46,330
Here, the view changes to find a target.

95
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:49,827
It is not the same mechanic as the puzzle, but both raise the

96
00:05:49,843 --> 00:05:52,650
question of reading where the view is facing after a change.

97
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:56,630
This footage alone cannot establish that a particular transition is comfortable.

98
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:59,307
Observe the orientation change and the remaining cues before

99
00:05:59,323 --> 00:06:02,425
considering choices in how the transition is presented.

100
00:06:09,150 --> 00:06:13,200
A gradual transition can allow time to follow the movement.

101
00:06:13,410 --> 00:06:17,320
A longer-moving image can also be less comfortable for someone else.

102
00:06:17,530 --> 00:06:20,040
One transition cannot represent everyone.

103
00:06:20,430 --> 00:06:25,160
An immediate change shortens the moving interval but
may make the arrival orientation harder to read.

104
00:06:25,290 --> 00:06:28,820
Motion duration and spatial understanding are separate questions.

105
00:06:28,970 --> 00:06:31,240
These two paths are explanatory diagrams.

106
00:06:31,390 --> 00:06:36,047
They do not claim that the game footage we just
watched implements these transition options.

107
00:06:36,063 --> 00:06:40,620
Consider both a choice to reduce transition motion
and cues for reading the arrival direction.

108
00:06:40,830 --> 00:06:47,150
When checking comfort, change one effect at a time
and allow players to stop or revert immediately.

109
00:06:47,867 --> 00:06:50,097
This is another cleaning interval around the slide.

110
00:06:50,447 --> 00:06:52,494
Can you find the surface reached

111
00:06:52,510 --> 00:06:54,237
by the spray while the view turns?

112
00:06:54,587 --> 00:06:58,537
The floor, side surface and nearby structure occupy different positions.

113
00:06:59,127 --> 00:07:02,677
When a new area comes into view, background lines move too.

114
00:07:03,247 --> 00:07:06,094
After the view settles, the relationship between the spray

115
00:07:06,110 --> 00:07:08,217
and the surface being cleaned should be readable again.

116
00:07:08,627 --> 00:07:10,717
Now look at the nearby planks and posts.

117
00:07:11,147 --> 00:07:14,154
Understanding the task requires seeing which surface the

118
00:07:14,170 --> 00:07:17,194
tool points at, as well as seeing the nozzle itself.

119
00:07:17,210 --> 00:07:21,057
That relationship should remain after changing the aiming mode.

120
00:07:21,407 --> 00:07:24,774
Reducing camera motion while obscuring the target or confusing

121
00:07:24,790 --> 00:07:27,677
the aiming position can introduce another control problem.

122
00:07:27,927 --> 00:07:29,654
What these shots directly show is the

123
00:07:29,670 --> 00:07:32,477
position of tool, surface and view.

124
00:07:32,767 --> 00:07:36,297
Do not confuse them with measurements of a player’s symptoms or comfort.

125
00:07:36,547 --> 00:07:39,017
Make the review questions specific.

126
00:07:39,387 --> 00:07:42,994
Beyond asking whether the image moves less, ask whether the cleaning target is

127
00:07:43,010 --> 00:07:45,867
visible and whether aiming remains readable after moving to another surface.

128
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:55,990
An option is less useful when it is hard to find.

129
00:07:56,260 --> 00:07:59,847
Make camera and aiming choices recognizable in

130
00:07:59,863 --> 00:08:02,770
initial settings and accessible again during play.

131
00:08:03,020 --> 00:08:06,290
Explain both what changes and what remains available.

132
00:08:06,540 --> 00:08:10,567
For example, treating tool aiming separately from view rotation

133
00:08:10,583 --> 00:08:14,190
still leaves the ability to look in another direction available.

134
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:17,090
Check saving and restoring defaults in practice.

135
00:08:17,380 --> 00:08:23,750
If values change after restarting or switching input devices,
players may have to find the same choice repeatedly.

136
00:08:24,160 --> 00:08:26,850
These checks verify how the camera options operate.

137
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:33,450
Comfort requires separate individual feedback; testing
should not become an exercise in enduring discomfort.

138
00:08:34,450 --> 00:08:37,820
Finally, watch another face of the playground structure being cleaned.

139
00:08:38,070 --> 00:08:40,817
While following a nearby surface, first

140
00:08:40,833 --> 00:08:43,400
identify where the nozzle and spray point.

141
00:08:43,430 --> 00:08:47,840
Moving around after cleaning part of a surface
changes the arrangement of posts and planks.

142
00:08:48,150 --> 00:08:50,560
Repositioning reveals another face; aiming

143
00:08:50,850 --> 00:08:53,520
then continues the task on that face.

144
00:08:53,890 --> 00:08:57,060
In the shot aimed at an overhead crossbar, the camera looks upward too.

145
00:08:57,230 --> 00:08:59,997
Distinguish moments when the whole background moves

146
00:09:00,013 --> 00:09:02,277
from moments when the tool moves around a target.

147
00:09:02,293 --> 00:09:05,137
The Aim Mode idea was to separate aiming from

148
00:09:05,153 --> 00:09:07,640
viewing direction during this kind of task.

149
00:09:08,130 --> 00:09:10,337
It does not mean eliminating every

150
00:09:10,353 --> 00:09:12,360
movement or everyone’s discomfort.

151
00:09:12,730 --> 00:09:14,617
When reviewing an example, first

152
00:09:14,633 --> 00:09:16,680
record what moves in each shot.

153
00:09:17,070 --> 00:09:20,457
Distinguishing necessary travel, aiming changes and added

154
00:09:20,473 --> 00:09:23,340
presentation makes the available choices more concrete.

155
00:09:23,730 --> 00:09:27,087
Here we observed an actual cleaning task continuing across different views.

156
00:09:27,103 --> 00:09:30,997
As a developer, you can now ask whether the same

157
00:09:31,013 --> 00:09:33,620
target remains readable with another control method.

158
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:43,750
The point is not to stop the camera unconditionally.

159
00:09:43,940 --> 00:09:50,410
Separate necessary viewpoint movement, tool aiming and
added presentation, then identify what players can control.

160
00:09:50,580 --> 00:09:56,450
In actual game shots, we examined what moves; in the
diagrams, what responsibilities could be separated.

161
00:09:56,720 --> 00:10:00,970
Check that the target and current orientation
remain readable after changing an option.

162
00:10:01,260 --> 00:10:04,407
Games require different controls, and people respond differently.

163
00:10:04,423 --> 00:10:08,187
Instead of promising one correct value, provide understandable

164
00:10:08,203 --> 00:10:11,990
choices, a way to revert and feedback from actual play.

165
00:10:12,240 --> 00:10:16,250
In your own game, start by separating the
responsibilities of the camera and aiming.

166
00:10:16,540 --> 00:10:22,360
To learn this kind of implementation together, see the
programming coaching link in the description and ending.
